<body>

<div class="container">

<h1>PingCraft — 4-Minute Video Demo Script</h1>
<p class="subtitle">Natural, conversational presentation script • Total reading time: ~3:30 to 4:15 min (~480 words)</p>

<div class="meta-box">
  <table style="width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse;">
    <tr>
      <td style="width: 50%;"><strong>Presenter:</strong> Prakrit Sangwan</td>
      <td style="width: 50%;"><strong>Target Duration:</strong> 3:30 &ndash; 4:30 Minutes</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Live App URL:</strong> <a href="https://pingcraft-gamma.vercel.app">https://pingcraft-gamma.vercel.app</a></td>
      <td><strong>GitHub Repo:</strong> <a href="https://github.com/Prakrit-Sangwan2402/pingcraft">pingcraft (main)</a></td>
    </tr>
  </table>
</div>

<h2>1. Introduction &amp; Login (0:00 &ndash; 0:45)</h2>

<div class="action">[ACTION: Screen on https://pingcraft-gamma.vercel.app at the Sign In page]</div>

<p class="spoken">
&ldquo;Hi everyone, I&rsquo;m Prakrit Sangwan, and today I&rsquo;m showing you PingCraft, an API health monitoring and incident management platform I built using the MERN stack.
</p>

<p class="spoken">
APIs often fail silently, and developers usually don&rsquo;t know until users complain. PingCraft solves this by pinging endpoints in the background, measuring real-time latency, and automatically triggering incidents when services go down.
</p>

<p class="spoken">
You can create a new account here with email and password, which uses bcrypt hashing on the backend. Since I already have an account, let me log in.&rdquo;
</p>

<div class="action">[ACTION: Type credentials and click &lsquo;Sign In&rsquo;]</div>

<p class="spoken">
&ldquo;Once I log in, you can see my username right here at the top corner.
Behind the scenes, the backend generates a signed JWT token on login. The frontend stores this token in React state and local storage, and attaches it as a Bearer header to authenticate every API call.&rdquo;
</p>

<h2>2. Dashboard &amp; Instant Probe with SSRF Defense (0:45 &ndash; 1:40)</h2>

<div class="action">[ACTION: Land on the Cockpit Dashboard]</div>

<p class="spoken">
&ldquo;Here on the main dashboard, we get an instant overview: total monitors, operational services, degraded endpoints, and active outages.
</p>

<p class="spoken">
Before setting up background jobs, PingCraft has an Instant Probe feature to test any endpoint immediately without saving it to the database.&rdquo;
</p>

<div class="action">[ACTION: Click &lsquo;Instant Probe&rsquo; button. Modal opens.]</div>

<p class="spoken">
&ldquo;Because users can enter any URL, we built in SSRF protection to stop attackers from probing internal servers.
Let&rsquo;s test it: if I try to ping localhost or a private cloud IP...&rdquo;
</p>

<div class="action">[ACTION: Enter http://localhost:5000/api/health and click &lsquo;Execute Probe&rsquo;]</div>

<p class="spoken">
&ldquo;...it&rsquo;s blocked immediately by our backend validator before opening any connection.
Now let&rsquo;s test a real public endpoint, like GitHub&rsquo;s API.&rdquo;
</p>

<div class="action">[ACTION: Enter https://api.github.com and click &lsquo;Execute Probe&rsquo;]</div>

<p class="spoken">
&ldquo;And in just a few milliseconds, we get the exact response time, HTTP status code, and payload size.&rdquo;
</p>

<h2>3. Monitor Metrics &amp; Telemetry (1:40 &ndash; 2:30)</h2>

<div class="action">[ACTION: Close modal, click on a monitor row to open Monitor Detail]</div>

<p class="spoken">
&ldquo;To monitor an endpoint continuously, you can click &lsquo;Add Monitor&rsquo; and set your URL, check frequency&mdash;like every 15 or 30 seconds&mdash;and failure thresholds.
</p>

<p class="spoken">
Opening an active monitor shows detailed telemetry:
</p>
<ul class="spoken">
  <li>Rolling 24-hour and 7-day uptime percentages.</li>
  <li>A latency trend chart over time, along with the 95th percentile (p95) response time.</li>
  <li>And down here is the ping history log, showing the exact timestamp and status for every probe.</li>
  <li>You can also pause any monitor anytime with one click.&rdquo;</li>
</ul>

<h2>4. Incident War Room &amp; Auto-Recovery (2:30 &ndash; 3:20)</h2>

<div class="action">[ACTION: Click &lsquo;Incident War Room&rsquo; in top navbar]</div>

<p class="spoken">
&ldquo;Next is the Incident War Room.
</p>

<p class="spoken">
When an endpoint fails consecutive checks, it flips from Degraded to Outage, and the system automatically creates an incident record. We use database constraints to make sure a monitor never gets duplicate active incidents.
</p>

<p class="spoken">
From here, responders can track the incident: moving it from Triggered, to Investigating, to Mitigating, while leaving timeline notes.&rdquo;
</p>

<div class="action">[ACTION: Click an active incident and click &lsquo;Acknowledge &amp; Investigate&rsquo;]</div>

<p class="spoken">
&ldquo;Every update is logged in an audit timeline.
</p>

<p class="spoken">
Best of all: when the service recovers and passes checks again, PingCraft automatically resolves the incident, logs the total downtime, and marks the service operational without any manual intervention.&rdquo;
</p>

<h2>5. Public Status Page &amp; Tech Stack (3:20 &ndash; 4:10)</h2>

<div class="action">[ACTION: Open Public Status Page tab /#/status/api-status]</div>

<p class="spoken">
&ldquo;For end users and customers, PingCraft provides a Public Status Page. This page is completely public, requires no login, reveals no sensitive tokens, and shows real-time uptime and active incidents.
</p>

<div class="action">[ACTION: Switch to Terminal showing 'npm test' passing]</div>

<p class="spoken">
Under the hood, the project is built on the MERN stack:
</p>
<ul class="spoken">
  <li>React 18, Vite, and Tailwind CSS deployed on Vercel.</li>
  <li>Node.js and Express deployed on Render.</li>
  <li>MongoDB Atlas with Mongoose for indexing and data storage.</li>
  <li>A background scheduler running parallel synthetic probes.</li>
</ul>

<p class="spoken">
To ensure reliability, the backend includes an automated test suite. Right here in the terminal, all 35 tests covering SSRF security, metrics, and incident workflows are passing cleanly.
</p>

<p class="spoken">
Both the live link and GitHub repository are in the description. Thank you!&rdquo;
</p>

<div class="tip-box">
  <strong>Recording Checklist:</strong>
  <ul style="margin: 4px 0 0 16px; padding: 0;">
    <li>Keep speaking pace steady and natural (you have ~480 words, giving you plenty of time).</li>
    <li>Fullscreen browser with F11.</li>
    <li>Submit video link at <a href="https://forms.gle/sBy4sfqyiRG2Zpom9">https://forms.gle/sBy4sfqyiRG2Zpom9</a>.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

</div>

